---
/**
 * /privacy/ — deliberately minimal and factual.
 *
 * It describes exactly what is collected today and nothing more; it is not a
 * general-purpose privacy policy and does not pretend to be.
 *
 * ── ROUND 24 CORRECTED FOUR STATEMENTS THAT THE CODE CONTRADICTED ─────────
 * Each was written when it was true and went stale as the site grew, which is
 * the failure mode a short page is supposed to prevent and did not:
 *
 *  1. "We do not collect your name, street address, or phone number. We do not
 *     ask for them anywhere on this site today." — /start/ has collected a
 *     first name and email since Round 1 (`projects.first_name`), the
 *     contractor-request step collects a phone number
 *     (`contractor_requests.phone`), and this page's own account section, two
 *     screens below, described storing a street address. It contradicted the
 *     code AND itself.
 *  2. "We use privacy-respecting page analytics." Nothing analytics-related
 *     renders unless PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID is set, and it is not set — so
 *     the page claimed a practice that was not happening. When it is set the
 *     tool is Google Analytics 4, which is worth naming rather than
 *     characterising.
 *  3. Two processors were never disclosed at all: Resend receives an email
 *     address to deliver each message, and a Slack webhook receives a ZIP and
 *     a timestamp on signup (`lib/ops/leadMessage.ts`, LEAD_DETAIL = "zip" —
 *     no email, no address).
 *  4. "It is not a flag on a row we keep; the records are deleted." True of
 *     everything the delete touches, but three records outlive it by design
 *     and the page did not say so.
 *
 * ── THE SERVICE-PROVIDERS SECTION IS WRITTEN IN THE FUTURE TENSE ON PURPOSE
 * The lead handoff does not exist. No tool is gated, nothing is shared with
 * any company, and describing an unbuilt mechanism in the present tense would
 * be the same class of error as the four above. It is published now because
 * the disclosure has to exist BEFORE the first handoff, not alongside it.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const title = "Privacy — What We Collect and Why | Texas Home Intelligence";
const description =
  "What Texas Home Intelligence collects, why, how long we keep it, and how to have it removed. " +
  "We collect an email address only when you ask to be told when the home dashboard launches.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Privacy", url: "/privacy/" }]} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <span class="eyebrow">PRIVACY</span>
      <h1>What we collect and why</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Texas Home Intelligence publishes public data about areas, not about people. Reading
        anything on this site requires no account, and the data pages and ZIP dashboards store
        nothing about you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:760px;">
      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        One thing: if you ask to be told when the home dashboard is ready, we store the
        <strong>email address you enter</strong> and the <strong>ZIP code you were viewing</strong>,
        along with a record of when you gave consent and which page you gave it on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two other parts of the site ask for more, and only when you use them. A
        <strong>home dashboard account</strong> holds an email address, a ZIP, and — only if you
        tick the box for it — a street address; that is described in full further down. The
        <strong>project brief</strong> at <a href="/start/">/start/</a> asks for a
        <strong>first name and an email address</strong>, and its optional contractor-request step
        asks for a <strong>phone number</strong>. Nothing on this site asks for a payment detail,
        a date of birth, or a government identifier.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we collect it</h2>
      <p>
        To send you one message when the home dashboard launches. The ZIP tells us which metro to
        mention. That is the entire purpose.
      </p>

      <h2>Nothing is stored unless you consent</h2>
      <p>
        The signup form has a consent checkbox, and it is not decorative: the server refuses the
        submission without it, and the database itself rejects any record that does not carry
        consent. If you fill in the box and change your mind before ticking it, nothing about you
        reaches us.
      </p>

      <h2>We do not sell your data</h2>
      <p>
        We do not sell or rent your email address to anyone, and we are not running an advertising
        business against it. We have never passed a homeowner's details to a company offering to
        do work, and we will not start doing it silently — the section below says exactly what
        would change and when.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two services we pay to run the site do see some of your information, because they cannot
        do their job without it. Both are named below.
      </p>

      <h2>How to be removed</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:privacy@texashomeintelligence.com">privacy@texashomeintelligence.com</a>
        and ask, and we will delete your record. You do not have to explain why, and you do not
        need an account to ask.
      </p>

      <h2>Services we use to run the site</h2>
      <p>Three, and this is all of them.</p>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.25em;">
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site and holds the database. Everything described
          on this page is stored there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resend</strong> delivers our email. To send you a sign-in link or a weekly
          summary, we hand Resend <strong>your email address and the message</strong> — one
          recipient at a time, never a list. It is a delivery service, not a marketing platform,
          and it does not get your address, your reminders, or anything else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Slack</strong> tells us when someone signs up, so we know the site is working.
          That notification contains <strong>the ZIP code and the time</strong> and nothing else —
          not your email address, not your street address, not your name.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Page analytics are currently switched off.</strong> No analytics script loads on
        any page of this site right now. If we turn them on, the tool will be Google Analytics 4
        and this page will say so before it happens. Either way we do not send your email address,
        your ZIP, your street address, or anything you typed to an analytics provider.
      </p>

      <h2 id="your-account">If you have an account</h2>
      <p>
        A home dashboard account holds a little more, and only what it needs:
      </p>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.25em;">
        <li><strong>Your email address</strong> &mdash; how you sign in. There is no password: we email a link that works once and expires in fifteen minutes, so there is no password of yours for us to store or lose. Condition alerts appear on your dashboard when you are signed in; we do not currently email them.</li>
        <li><strong>Your ZIP code</strong> &mdash; which county's readings your dashboard shows.</li>
        <li><strong>Your street address, only if you tick the box for it</strong> &mdash; it is optional, it is stored separately from the rest of your account, and everything on the dashboard works without it. It does not buy a reading about your specific house; every figure we publish is county- or metro-level.</li>
        <li><strong>Your reminders and what you have marked done</strong> &mdash; the dates you entered and the dates you completed things.</li>
        <li><strong>Which alerts you switched on.</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We do not track which pages you read while signed in, and we never send your email
        address, your street address, or anything you typed into a reminder to an analytics
        provider.
      </p>

      <h2 id="service-providers">Companies we introduce you to</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing on this site does this yet.</strong> No tool asks for a job today, nothing
        has been shared with any company, and there is no list of companies. This section is here
        because we are building it, and a description of what will happen to your information
        belongs on this page before it happens rather than after.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is what it will be. Some tools will end by asking whether you would like us to find
        companies who can do the work. <strong>That question is a separate step with its own
        button.</strong> Using a tool, reading its result, or having an account never triggers it.
        If you do not answer it, nothing leaves this site.
      </p>

      <h3>What we would share, and with whom</h3>
      <p>
        If you say yes, we would send a company <strong>your first name, your email address, the
        job you described, and the area you are in</strong> &mdash; and your phone number only if
        you gave us one. We would send the description of the work you wrote. We would not send
        your dashboard, your reminders, what you have marked done, or anything you entered
        elsewhere on this site.
      </p>

      <h3>The companies do not already exist</h3>
      <p>
        This is the part most worth being clear about, because it is the opposite of how these
        services usually work. <strong>We do not have a network of companies waiting.</strong> When
        you ask, we go and look for companies that do that particular job in your area, and we
        approach them. That takes time &mdash; it is people making enquiries, not a form hitting a
        database. Companies we find this way may be ones we contact again for later requests.
      </p>

      <h3>What an introduction is not</h3>
      <p>
        We do not inspect your home, diagnose the problem, or supervise the work. We do not check
        licences, insurance, bonding, or complaint history, and we do not rate or rank anyone.
        <strong>An introduction is not a recommendation and not an endorsement.</strong> We are
        telling a company that you are looking; deciding whether to hire them is entirely yours,
        and so is asking them for their licence, their insurance, and their references.
      </p>

      <h3>Changing your mind</h3>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:privacy@texashomeintelligence.com">privacy@texashomeintelligence.com</a>
        and we will stop. If we have not sent your details to anyone yet, nothing goes out. If we
        already have, we will tell you which companies received them and ask each of them to
        delete what we sent &mdash; and we will say plainly that we can ask, not compel.
        <strong>Information already in someone else's hands is out of ours</strong>, and anyone who
        tells you otherwise is overpromising. Their own privacy practices are theirs, not ours.
      </p>

      <h3>How long we keep it</h3>
      <p>
        A request record &mdash; what you asked for, when, and who we introduced you to &mdash;
        is kept for <strong>two years</strong>, then deleted. We keep it that long so that if you
        come back and ask what happened, we can tell you. You can have it deleted sooner by
        asking. Deleting your dashboard account does not automatically delete a request record,
        because the two are separate; ask for both if you want both gone.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
      <p>
        There is a delete option on your dashboard. It removes your account, your email address,
        your address if you gave one, your home profile, every reminder and its history, and your
        alert settings &mdash; and it signs you out everywhere. It is not a flag on a row we keep;
        the records are deleted.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also remove just your street address and keep the account, since the address is
        stored on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Three things outlive that delete, and they are worth naming rather than leaving
        you to discover.</strong> If you asked to be told when the dashboard launched, that
        signup is a separate record and is not removed by deleting an account &mdash; ask and we
        will delete it. If an email to you ever bounced or you marked one as spam, we keep the
        address on a do-not-send list, because the alternative is emailing you again. And a
        project brief you started at <a href="/start/">/start/</a> is stored separately from
        accounts entirely. Ask for any of them and they go.
      </p>

      <h2>What this page is not</h2>
      <p>
        This is a plain description of what the site does today, kept deliberately short so it
        stays accurate. It is not legal advice and not a comprehensive policy. As Texas Home
        Intelligence adds features that handle more of your information &mdash; a home profile, or
        alerts tied to an address &mdash; this page will be rewritten before those ship, not after.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
